package Libraries.Compute.Hardware.Shaders

/*
The DoubleNumber32ShaderCode class gives the GLSL a compute shader needs to
work with Quorum's 64-bit numbers on any graphics card, in
HardwareCompute's DOUBLE_NUMBER_32 format: each number as two 32-bit
numbers, high and low (a vec2 in GLSL), about 48 bits. Graphics cards
without 64-bit numbers, such as Apple's, get close to 64-bit precision this
way.

    GetBufferDeclaration   the storage buffer, "buff", as 32-bit numbers
    GetLoadAndStore        loadNumber(i) and storeNumber(i, value): number
                           i of the buffer, 32-bit numbers 2i and 2i + 1
    GetArithmetic          multiplyNumbers(a, b) and addNumbers(a, b), and
                           the error-free steps they are made of

The arithmetic keeps each operation's rounding error: twoProduct uses fma,
which gives a product's error exactly, and every sum is renormalized so the
low part stays small. Every 32-bit number in it is marked precise (SPIR-V
NoContraction), so a compiler running with fast math, as MoltenVK does for
Metal, may not reorder, simplify or fuse it; without that, b - (s - a) can
be "simplified" to 0 and the shader quietly loses half its precision.

Attribute: Example

use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.Shaders.DoubleNumber32ShaderCode

// squares every number in the buffer
DoubleNumber32ShaderCode pairs
text code = "#version 450
" + pairs:GetBufferDeclaration(0) + "
layout (local_size_x = 64) in;
" + pairs:GetLoadAndStore() + pairs:GetArithmetic() + "
void main()
{
    uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    vec2 value = loadNumber(i);
    storeNumber(i, multiplyNumbers(value, value));
}
"
*/
class DoubleNumber32ShaderCode
    /*
    This action returns the GLSL for the storage buffer the other actions'
    code uses: named buff, read and written as 32-bit numbers (buff.data).

    Attribute: Parameter binding the buffer's binding in descriptor set 0.

    Attribute: Returns the GLSL declaration.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.Shaders.DoubleNumber32ShaderCode

    DoubleNumber32ShaderCode pairs
    text declaration = pairs:GetBufferDeclaration(0)
    */
    action GetBufferDeclaration(integer binding) returns text
        return "
layout (std430, set=0, binding=" + binding + ") buffer srcBuf {
    float data[];
} buff;
"
    end

    /*
    This action returns the GLSL functions loadNumber(i), which reads
    number i of the buffer as a vec2 (32-bit numbers 2i and 2i + 1, high
    and low), and storeNumber(i, value), which writes one. It needs the
    buffer from GetBufferDeclaration.

    Attribute: Returns the GLSL functions.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.Shaders.DoubleNumber32ShaderCode

    DoubleNumber32ShaderCode pairs
    text code = pairs:GetBufferDeclaration(0) + pairs:GetLoadAndStore()
    */
    action GetLoadAndStore returns text
        return "
vec2 loadNumber(uint index) {
    return vec2(buff.data[2u * index], buff.data[2u * index + 1u]);
}

void storeNumber(uint index, vec2 value) {
    buff.data[2u * index]      = value.x;
    buff.data[2u * index + 1u] = value.y;
}
"
    end

    /*
    This action returns the GLSL functions for arithmetic on numbers held
    as two 32-bit numbers: multiplyNumbers(a, b) and addNumbers(a, b),
    each taking and returning a vec2 (high, low), and the error-free steps
    they are made of (quickTwoSum, twoSum, twoProduct). Each keeps its
    rounding error, and every step is marked precise.

    Attribute: Returns the GLSL functions.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.Shaders.DoubleNumber32ShaderCode

    DoubleNumber32ShaderCode pairs
    text code = pairs:GetArithmetic()
    */
    action GetArithmetic returns text
        return "
// a + b as a sum and its exact rounding error, when |a| >= |b|
vec2 quickTwoSum(float a, float b) {
    precise float s = a + b;
    precise float e = b - (s - a);
    return vec2(s, e);
}

// a + b as a sum and its exact rounding error, for any a and b
vec2 twoSum(float a, float b) {
    precise float s = a + b;
    precise float v = s - a;
    precise float e = (a - (s - v)) + (b - v);
    return vec2(s, e);
}

// a * b as a product and its exact rounding error
vec2 twoProduct(float a, float b) {
    precise float p = a * b;
    precise float e = fma(a, b, -p);
    return vec2(p, e);
}

vec2 multiplyNumbers(vec2 a, vec2 b) {
    vec2 p = twoProduct(a.x, b.x);
    precise float crossTerms = a.x * b.y + a.y * b.x;
    precise float e = p.y + crossTerms;
    return quickTwoSum(p.x, e);
}

vec2 addNumbers(vec2 a, vec2 b) {
    vec2 s = twoSum(a.x, b.x);
    precise float e = s.y + (a.y + b.y);
    return quickTwoSum(s.x, e);
}
"
    end
end
